(project pricklypear-migrations
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: migrations")
 (status partial)
 (* agent note (|2026-08-02 status reconcile: applied-by-image+additive-only match ops; branching-interaction stays partial (type_defs merge/schema_vers messaging).|) *)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section migrations

  (doc "Schema evolution: image-applied, additive-only, schema authority in main")
  (status partial)
  (implements
   migrations/001_branches.sql
   migrations/002_auth.sql
   migrations/003_data.sql
   migrations/004_bible_ref_index.sql
   migrations/008_agent_users.sql
   migrations/010_notes_tree.sql
   migrations/013_data_user_scoping.sql
   migrations/014_guestbook_shared.sql
   migrations/015_jobs.sql)
  (verify
   (build "dune build image")
   (smoke "image boots against a fresh database; schema_migrations records 001..007; re-boot is a no-op"))
  (subsection applied-by-image
   (doc "The image owns schema state before it serves")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02: migrations/001–012 + schema_migrations; apply via scripts/dev.sh and deploy hooks before serve (AGENTS.md). Idempotent SQL files.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         The IMAGE runs migrations. On boot it applies any
         pending migrations in version order, and only then
         starts serving. (scripts/dev.sh applies them
         directly in the dev habitat.) Images assume "the
         schema is current" once boot completes.

         Migration source: SQL files in migrations/
         (version-numbered). Tracked in the
         schema_migrations table; application is
         idempotent (IF NOT EXISTS guards), so re-applying
         is a no-op.

         Initial migrations:
           001_branches.sql  — code store tables
             (branches, function_defs, type_defs,
             view_bindings, sessions, schema_migrations)
           002_auth.sql      — auth tables
             (users, credentials, auth_sessions, auth_log;
             system_keys is inert legacy)
           003_data.sql      — shared data_rows
           004_bible_ref_index.sql — bible lookup indexes
           005_ai_sessions.sql — agent conversation store
             (ai_sessions, ai_messages; NOT auth_sessions).
             Spec: ai-authoring.agent-portal.
           006_route_visibility.sql — view_bindings.visibility
             text NOT NULL DEFAULT 'private' (public|private).
             Spec: auth.route-visibility. Additive column only;
             existing routes stay private.
           007_media.sql — media_blobs (bytea object store for
             agent/browser image upload). Spec: storage.media.
             Shared across branches like data_rows; ownership
             via author column. Additive table only.
           008_agent_users.sql — agent user rows / allow flags.
           009_tree.sql — one-tree substrate (tree_nodes,
             tree_branches, bidirectional deferred mirrors,
             tree_* read views, tree_verify). Spec: one-tree.
           012_code_history.sql — code_history table +
             BEFORE UPDATE/DELETE triggers on function_defs,
             type_defs, view_bindings. Append-only before-image
             history for every code-row write.
             Spec: branching.code-history.
           015_jobs.sql — durable background jobs (jobs,
             job_steps, schedules) as a DEDICATED table (not
             data_rows/tree nodes): the runner's SKIP LOCKED
             claim protocol needs real typed status/run_at
             columns + partial jobs_claim_idx; kernel rows
             belong to the migration tier. Spec: jobs.
           018_tree_versions.sql — ADDITIVE versioned-leaf table
             for tree-time (branch_id, path, gen) append-only
             history + covering index + tree_generations row,
             gen-range partitioned for retention GC, tombstone
             representation. DESIGNED in tree-time.migration-018
             (campaign 5.0 plan pass); the file lands with 5.1
             (make versioned-leaves) and is not yet tracked.
             Spec: tree-time.
           019_tree_version_shadow.sql — tree-time Phase 1
             dual-write shadow: AFTER trigger on tree_nodes
             appends a tree_versions row per write (allocating
             the head gen per path class) + tombstone on delete.
             Additive; Spec: tree-time.versioned-leaves.
           020_tree_version_echo_guard.sql — tree-time mirror-
             echo guard: tree_version_append() REFINED to make a
             no-op mirror bounce (same content body/author/kind/
             type) return before allocating a gen or inserting a
             version row, so one logical write is never double-
             counted. Additive CREATE OR REPLACE; Spec:
             tree-time.versioned-leaves.
           021_tree_versions_reconcile.sql — tree-time head
             reconciliation tool: tree_versions_reconcile_heads()
             heals the one non-head-equivalent class (a shadowed
             LIVE row whose newest version is a TOMBSTONE —
             delete-then-recreate whose re-create did not re-
             shadow) by appending the live content as a fresh
             head, and repairs the branch/data head counters to
             the max live gen. Additive CREATE OR REPLACE;
             runner: scripts/reconcile_tree_version_head.sh.
             Spec: tree-time.versioned-leaves.
           022_tree_retention.sql — tree-time.gc retention
             worker: tree_retention_policy (policy AS HABITAT
             DATA, path_class code|data, type_name NULL = whole
             class, UNIQUE(path_class,type_name)) + tree_
             versions_retain(path_class, keep_windows): computes
             the whole-class floor from the live head gen,
             CHECKPOINTs the newest in-retained-past state of
             every fully-retired path to a row at the floor
             boundary, then DROP PARTITIONs every lazy gen-window
             entirely below the floor — never the base gen_0
             window. Additive + idempotent + image-applied; the
             worker runs on the jobs runner (boot.ml,
             migration-015) gated by wall-clock.
             Spec: tree-time.gc.

         A migration in flight blocks serving until it
         completes (or fails). This guarantees the image
         always sees a consistent schema. Migration failure
         aborts boot and requires manual intervention — no
         half-applied state. (The additive-only policy makes
         failure recovery simpler.)
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection additive-only
   (doc "Schema changes can ADD, not remove or rename")
   (status implemented)
   (verify
    (build "grep -rE 'DROP|RENAME|ALTER COLUMN TYPE' migrations/ = 0 matches")
    (smoke "all migrations idempotently re-appliable"))

   (details
    (doc (|
         Schema evolution policy for type_defs in main:
         ADDITIVE ONLY. You can:
           - Add a new type (new table)
           - Add a new field to an existing type
             (ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN, with NOT NULL
             forbidden unless a DEFAULT is specified)

         You CANNOT:
           - Remove a field (no DROP COLUMN)
           - Rename a field (no RENAME)
           - Change a field's type (no ALTER COLUMN TYPE
             except widening)

         WHY:
         - Additive changes are backward-compatible with
           running images. An image running old code that
           doesn't know about a new field still works; it
           just ignores the column.
         - Removing/renaming breaks running images that
           reference the field, with no compile-time error
           to catch it (Nopales is dynamic).
         - The cost is cruft accumulation: dead fields
           pile up over time. Tradeoff accepted for
           correctness.

         When a field truly must go: a manual migration
         (off-cycle, admin-run during scheduled downtime)
         that also updates affected branch code. This is
         rare and treated as an incident, not routine.

         This policy is set early because retrofitting it
         later (after non-additive migrations exist) is
         expensive. It is a foundation constraint.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection branching-interaction
   (doc "How migrations interact with branches")
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02 confirm: shared tables + additive-only policy live; type_defs proposal→main migration path and schema_vers messaging still incomplete.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Migrations only apply to the SHARED data tables,
         governed by main's type_defs (which are
         merge-governed into main).

         Branches CANNOT migrate data tables. A branch
         can PROPOSE a type change in its type_defs, but
         the underlying table doesn't change until that
         proposal is merged to main and the image applies
         the resulting migration at next boot.

         A branch running code that references a field
         added in main but not yet handled by the branch's
         own code: works (the column exists; branch code
         ignores it on write, reads null on read if no
         default).

         A branch running code that references a field
         that DOESN'T exist (because the branch's type_defs
         is behind main's): runtime error at the primitive
         layer. The user gets a clear message: "field X
         not in current main schema; merge your schema
         change or sync to main".

         Schema version is tracked per type (schema_vers
         in type_defs). Branches see the version they
         declared; main sees its current version. The
         primitive layer can validate against either at
         query time.
   |))
   )
  )
 )
)
